264
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
the Boy Scouts of America,

265
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
our Scouting organization,

266
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
has undertaken

267
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
a lengthy

268
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
and thorough examination

269
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
of its membership standards.

270
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
after this review,

271
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
our national executive board

272
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
has drafted a membership

273
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
standards resolution

274
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
that will be voted on on Thursday,

275
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
May 23, at the

276
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
BSA annual meeting.

277
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
there's approximately 1400

278
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
voting members representing

279
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
265 local councils

280
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
nationwide,

281
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
and they're going to vote on a resolution

282
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
that, in short,

283
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
removes the stipulation

284
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
that youth members

285
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
can be excluded from membership in the BSA

286
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
based solely

287
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
on their sexual orientation.

288
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
in this podcast, we're going to spend

289
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
a little bit of time

290
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
discussing this issue

291
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
and sharing with you

292
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
some of the information that the BSA

293
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
has published

294
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
for the voting members.

295
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
the reason that I am choosing to discuss this now

296
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
is that I think

297
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
that this is a historic moment

298
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
in the history of the BSA.

299
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
now, when I broach this subject

300
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
on the blog or on the podcast,

301
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
it is clear

302
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
everyone listening- and everyone reading

303
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
is not necessarily

304
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
going to be in agreement

305
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
with my opinions

306
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
or with the opinions of

307
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
our national executive committee.

308
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
the BSA has prepared

309
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
a voting member information packet

310
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
that's available as a pdf file.

311
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
if you'll go to

312
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
scoutmastercgcom,

313
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
you'll find the post

314
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
that I'm referring to and I'll make sure

315
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
to link it to this podcast

316
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
so that you can go and read that packet

317
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
yourself if you like,

318
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and it describes the voting process

319
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and includes the findings of the membership

320
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
standards review.

321
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
now, in addition to that

322
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
membership information packet,

323
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
our national president, Wayne Perry,

324
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
and a member of the national executive committee,

325
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
Nathan Rosenberg,

326
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
and our chief scout executive,

327
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
Wayne Brock,

328
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
hosted an in-depth discussion

329
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
of the BSA's membership standards

330
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
resolution

331
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
back at the end of April

332
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and they recorded this discussion

333
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
and it is available for viewing.

334
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
you'll find that video at

335
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
scoutmastercgcom.

336
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
we're all aware that this membership

337
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
standards review is a historic effort

338
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
on the part of the BSA.

339
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
no matter where you come down on this,

340
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
you can certainly say

341
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
thank you to the people who put their

342
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
hearts and minds and thousands

343
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
of volunteer hours into this

344
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
process in an honest

345
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
desire to do their best for our scouts.

346
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
we all owe them our appreciation.

347
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
the presentation

348
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
that I'm speaking of- the video presentation-

349
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
is a long one- it lasts about an

350
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
hour- but it's worth your time.

351
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
what I found particularly interesting

352
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
was a narrative of what happened at the

353
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
meeting of the committee assembled

354
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
to draft the membership resolution,

355
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
and this was related

356
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
by our national executive

357
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
committee vice president for human resources.

358
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
his name is Lyle Knight.

359
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
he said that, whatever your

360
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
personal opinion might be,

361
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
someone who was working on

362
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
this resolution

363
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
shared your opinion.

364
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
if you'll go and listen to that video

365
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and listen to our national leaders,

366
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
you're soon going to understand.

367
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
we're going to be choosing one of two

368
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
paths: we're going to choose to change

369
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
our membership standards or we're going to choose

370
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
to maintain them as they are right now.

371
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
there's a slide that was shared on the video

372
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
that I've been talking about

373
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
that kind of describes what happens

374
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
if we change

375
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and what happens if we don't change.

376
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
basically, what happens if

377
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
the membership policy changes

378
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
is it's projected

379
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
we're going to experience an initial

380
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
decline in membership

381
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
of around 15%

382
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
in both youth and adult members.

383
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
we're going to probably

384
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
experience a significant financial loss

385
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
nationally

386
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
and we'll possibly lose

387
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
a significant number of chartered partners.

388
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
that's a very sobering reality,

389
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
isn't it, and I know that it's causing us

390
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
a lot of angst

391
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and sadness

392
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
and distress.

393
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
as sobering as the projected results

394
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
changing the membership policy is,

395
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
the projected results of

396
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
not changing it

397
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
is probably even more dire.

398
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
the BSA has lost

399
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
nearly 30%

400
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
of our membership over the past

401
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
dozen or so years.

402
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
I'm sure that there are many reasons for that.

403
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
I know personally,

404
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
at least in our council.

405
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
we can attribute

406
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
a lot of this membership stagnation

407
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
and this loss of membership

408
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
to the current policy.

409
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
that's not going to be true

410
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
in every part of the country and I understand that.

411
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
but I can tell you from my own experience

412
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and the reactions that we get

413
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
when we're recruiting for Cub Scouts

414
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
that this policy

415
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
is a barrier to participation

416
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
for a number of people

417
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
that we talk to.

418
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
so not changing this membership policy

419
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
means that we're probably

420
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
going to continue to see this

421
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
loss of membership

422
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and see it accelerate.

423
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
we're going to see a continued erosion

424
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
of financial support

425
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
and, I think,

426
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
an accelerated marginalization

427
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
of the BSA.

428
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
so on the one hand, we know that

429
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
there's an immediate loss

430
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
and an immediate result from changing

431
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
the policy,

432
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
but the long-term results of not changing it

433
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
are even worse

434
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
than the immediate results

435
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
of changing it.

436
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
but it's still enough to think that

437
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
if we change this membership policy

438
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
it will immediately open the floodgates

439
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
and result in a significant increase

440
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
in membership.

441
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
perhaps the best case scenario

442
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
is that our rate of loss

443
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
slows down.

444
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
perhaps we'll see that loss level out

445
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
and we may see a modest increase

446
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
over the next several years.

447
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
I do agree with what

448
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
our national executive board

449
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
has projected:

450
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
the BSA is going to be

451
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
a significantly smaller

452
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and significantly less relevant

453
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
organization to the vast majority

454
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
of American families in years to come.

455
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
so I do join

456
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
the members of our national executive board

457
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
in calling on

458
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
support for the resolution

459
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
and, beyond that,

460
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
a spirit of cooperation.

461
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
no matter what happens this Thursday,

462
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
we're going to see some of

463
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
our valuable adult volunteers

464
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
and chartering organizations

465
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
decide that they can no longer

466
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
identify themselves with the BSA.

467
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
that is tragic

468
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and it's unfortunate.

469
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
I can understand the depth of emotion

470
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
involved in that kind of decision

471
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
and I've had discussions

472
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
via email with people

473
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
who are really feeling this

474
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
and are really having an internal

475
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
struggle with it.

476
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
despite all the wrangling and all the discussion

477
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and all the assumptions

478
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
that our national leadership,

479
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
over and over again

480
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
in their deliberations on this,

481
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
has emphasized, their actions

482
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
are in response to

483
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
one single concern:

484
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
what is the best course of action

485
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
for our scouts?

486
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
I believe they're earnestly trying to provide

487
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
the best answer that they can.

488
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
I believe that's a question

489
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
that all of us have to answer:

490
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
how do we best serve

491
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
our scouts

492
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
whenever? to answer that question

493
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
to the best of my ability.

494
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
any responsible volunteer

495
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
is endeavoring to answer that question

496
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
to the best of their ability.

497
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
on this particular issue,

498
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
we'll reach different conclusions,

499
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
but we ought to be able to find a way forward

500
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
that really is the best solution

501
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
for all of the scouts

502
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
we're serving now

503
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
and all of the scouts that we can

504
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
potentially serve in the future.

505
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
looking at the history of

506
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
scouting and

507
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
reading

508
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
what our founder,

509
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
Robert Baden-Powell, had to say

510
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
about it can help us

511
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
look forward a bit.

512
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
after scouting was founded,

513
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
it quickly spread around the world,

514
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
just spontaneously.

515
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Baden-Powell wrote this about

516
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
those years:

517
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
he said scouting was not a year old

518
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
before other countries had formed

519
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
their own branches.

520
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
outside training has spread to

521
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
42 different countries

522
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
around the world

523
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
and has proved its potentiality

524
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
as a factor in world peace.

525
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
now, at that time,

526
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
some urged Baden-Powell

527
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
that this spontaneous growth

528
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
was probably not a great idea.

529
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
you need to stop and control this

530
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
and patent your idea

531
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
so that other people in other countries

532
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
can't just pick it up

533
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
and start using it.

534
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
it's an exclusive movement

535
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
and it's going to become diluted and diminished

536
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
when people in other countries start using it.

537
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
you should have some control over that.

538
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
they may not get it right.

539
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
it's too unstable and too uncertain

540
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
just to let it go.

541
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
I think Baden-Powell sensed that

542
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
this spontaneous growth

543
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
of scouting was not really dangerous

544
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
and it didn't threaten to dilute

545
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
scouting and make it something that

546
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
he didn't intend it to be.

547
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
I think that he saw that this

548
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
spontaneity and the diversity that resulted from it

549
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
would in fact

550
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
become one of the greatest and strongest

551
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
things about scouting.

552
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
I think his vision evolved.

553
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
he was a military man

554
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
and he incorporated a lot of militaristic

555
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
ideas into scouting.

556
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
as scouting started to spread through the world,

557
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
he saw it

558
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
as a vehicle for world peace.

559
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
Baden-Powell would call

560
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
the first international scout gathering

561
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
in 1920

562
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
the first reunion of

563
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
the world's boyhood in the aftermath

564
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
of World War I.

565
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
after 10 days of camping

566
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
and working together in Olympia, London,

567
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
the scouts of more than 20 nations

568
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
met with Baden-Powell and they asked him

569
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
to make this appeal.

570
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
at the end of what would become known

571
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
as the first world jamboree,

572
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
he said this:

573
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
brother scouts, I ask you

574
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
to make a solemn choice.

575
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
differences exist between

576
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
people of the world in thought and temperament.

577
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
this jamboree has taught us

578
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
that, with mutual give and take,

579
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
under a common ideal,

580
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
sympathy and harmony are established.

581
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
if it be your will, therefore,

582
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
let us endeavor

583
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
to develop amongst our boys

584
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
such comradeship, through the

585
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
world wide scout spirit

586
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
of brotherhood, that peace

587
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
and good will may henceforth

588
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
reign among men.

589
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
are you willing to join me

590
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
in this high enterprise?

591
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
and the scouts that

592
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
heard this appeal responded

593
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
overwhelmingly

594
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
yes, we will.

595
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
in writing about this momentous occasion,

596
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
Baden-Powell quoted the Bengali poet

597
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
Rabindranath Tagore,

598
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
who wrote:

599
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
it's through brotherhood

600
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
and not through organization that the world

601
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
should be ordered. we are citizens

602
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
of the kingdom of ideas.

603
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
there will be men large enough

604
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
to see the human race as a whole

605
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
and who understand that the good

606
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
of the community

607
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
as a family does exist.

608
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
Baden-Powell added to this,

609
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
if we reflect that

610
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
we are all members of the human family

611
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
and we're only here on this earth

612
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
for a short span of life,

613
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
we realize that the petty differences

614
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
and fighting for little

615
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
selfish ends are out of

616
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
place in the creator's scheme.

617
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
Tagore's thought and

618
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
Baden-Powell's word

619
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
described the promise of scouting very simply:

620
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
we can all get along

621
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
with each other if we try.

622
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
some scouts in a patrol may want one thing

623
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
for dinner while they're camping and someone

624
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
another thing. what do they do? they discuss

625
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
and they debate and they arrive at a decision.

626
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
that really

627
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
kind of seemingly insignificant decision

628
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
made by a bunch of boys

629
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
is something that is going to echo

630
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
throughout their lives

631
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and throughout the world, with the message:

632
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
we can get along with each other

633
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
if we try,

634
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
scouting spread spontaneously.

635
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
and only then was it

636
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
clothed in the garments of tradition

637
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
and policy and rules and regulations.

638
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
if organizations grow too inflexible

639
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
and arthritic to suit

640
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
the vitality

641
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
of the ideas that created them,

642
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
they have to renew themselves,

643
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
they have to change

644
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
or they're going to crumble

645
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
and fall. now, naturally,

646
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
not everybody is

647
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
invigorated and energized by change.

648
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
there are those of us,

649
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
in every successive generation

650
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
in human history,

651
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
who, on reaching a certain age, think that the world

652
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
has become worse somehow. but I think

653
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
really. I think our best days are ahead.

654
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
I wouldn't exchange the time

655
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
that we're living in, with all its

656
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
problems and difficulties,

657
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
for any era in the past.

658
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
I don't think there's any perfect place

659
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
for us to return to in the past

660
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
and I don't think there's any ideal future ahead.

661
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
I think what we have

662
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
is we have now,

663
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
and I think that we're called on to be leaders

664
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
right now.

665
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
the scouting idea grows despite us.

666
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
we can't hold it back,

667
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
no matter how hard we try.

668
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
in these past few months, we've all been caught up

669
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
in working to define the future

670
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
of the Boy Scouts

671
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
of America.

672
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
we've argued, we've debated,

673
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
we've listened and talked

674
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
and now a decision is going to be made.

675
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
the decision is

676
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
just a start.

677
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
whatever is decided,

678
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
you know some of us are going to disagree

679
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
and some of us may leave.

680
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
change and innovation

681
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
are uncertain and dynamic

682
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
and unpredictable elements.

683
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
they challenge us, they upset us,

684
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
but we ought to welcome the idea

685
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
of change and we ought to work hard

686
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
to see what's next.

687
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
the scouting idea will continue on

688
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
to change the world one

689
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
scout at a time.

690
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Baden Powell's appeal

691
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
nearly a century ago

692
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
at that first world jamboree

693
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
is still alive to us today.

694
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
let us therefore endeavor

695
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
to develop amongst our scouts

696
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
such comradeship,

697
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
through the worldwide scout

698
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
spirit of brotherhood,

699
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
that peace and goodwill

700
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
can henceforth reign among men.

701
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
are you willing to join

702
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
this high enterprise?

703
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
we can all get along if we try.

704
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
we can all make this work,

705
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
wherever we end up at the end of this week,

706
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
let's resolve this,

707
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
that we're going to roll up our sleeves

708
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
and we're going to endeavor to work together

709
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
in the best way possible

710
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
to serve our scouts,

711
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
because that's the point of all of this, isn't it?

712
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
we can all get along

713
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
if we try

714
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:22,000
music.

